\end{table}

No curriculum schedule beats the simple NL baseline on OOD. The sandwich schedule gets close on ID (0.728) but still falls short on Laptop14. The fast-ramp + dependency variant gets closest to baseline OOD (0.523) but within noise. Curriculum learning optimises the learning trajectory for in-domain convergence but does not introduce domain-relevant variation.

\subsection{Aspect Masking}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Aspect masking results (NL format, 30 epochs). Masking at different fractions.}
\label{tab:masking}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Configuration} & \textbf{Rest14 (ID)} & \textbf{Laptop14 (OOD)} \\
\midrule
NL baseline & 0.722 & 0.525 \\
\midrule
Mask 10\% + dep. & 0.718 & 0.507 \\
Mask 25\% + dep. & 0.719 & 0.488 \\
Mask 50\% + dep. & 0.704 & 0.510 \\
Mask 25\% (no dep.) & 0.713 & 0.511 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Masking consistently hurts OOD. The premise was that forcing the model to predict aspects from context rather than by copying would improve generalisation to novel aspects. In practice, removing real aspect context from the input removes the very signal the model needs --- it learns to hallucinate plausible aspects from the training domain rather than recognising novel ones.

\subsection{Cross-Domain Data Mixing}

