Both augmentation fractions hurt performance across the board. Less augmentation means less damage, but even 20\% synthetic data degrades OOD from 0.525 to 0.486. The LLM-generated sentences introduce distributional mismatch (different sentence structures, tokenisation patterns) that the model learns as noise. This is not a dosage problem --- the direction is consistently negative.

\subsection{Decoding Strategies}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Decoding strategies evaluated on the NL + compact dep. checkpoint.}
\label{tab:decoding}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Rest14 (ID)} & \textbf{Laptop14 (OOD)} \\
\midrule
Greedy (default) & 0.708 & 0.541 \\
Beam search (k=4) & 0.713 & 0.530 \\
Sampling (t=0.7) & 0.699 & 0.477 \\
Sampling (t=0.9) & 0.672 & 0.446 \\
Voting (5$\times$, t=0.8, thresh=3) & 0.710 & 0.520 \\
Constrained decoding & 0.699 & 0.483 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Greedy decoding is optimal for OOD. All alternatives hurt. Temperature sampling introduces noise that compounds on OOD data where the model is already less certain. Beam search provides marginal ID gains but hurts OOD. Constrained decoding (FSM-based template enforcement) is unnecessary --- the NL format already provides strong implicit constraints during training, and the FSM restrictions fight the model's learned generation patterns.


