MvP (multi-task, 10 datasets) & $\sim$400M & 0.76 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The comparison highlights several findings:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Fine-tuned FLAN-T5-base (250M) outperforms all LLMs tested in few-shot (27--35B) on both ID and OOD, by large margins on OOD (0.525 vs 0.41 for the best LLM).
    \item Our naive RoBERTa baseline underperforms the fine-tuned T5 by 18 points ID and 14 points OOD --- the generative approach provides genuine architectural advantages for this task, not just better engineering.
    \item Published discriminative SOTA (BTF-CCL: 0.76) uses specialised architectures with GCN, contrastive learning, and boundary detection that our simple baseline does not implement. Our generative approach (0.72--0.73 ID) is competitive without any such specialisation.
    \item The published in-domain numbers for MvP (0.76) use multi-task training across 10 datasets. Our single-dataset MvP replication achieves 0.73 ID, suggesting that most of MvP's reported gain comes from multi-task data exposure rather than the multi-view mechanism itself.
\end{enumerate}


