XLM-R (classifier approach) performs best overall due to its multilingual pretraining, but the margin over T5 is small. The main takeaway is consistent with our English findings: fine-tuning a 250M model remains competitive with much larger models even in a language it was not primarily designed for.


\section{Error Analysis}

We ran detailed error analysis on 7 model variants (Rest14 test + Laptop14 test) to understand \textit{why} performance drops OOD and how error patterns differ between methods. Here we focus on the NL baseline (our best OOD model) to characterise the nature of out-of-domain failures.

\subsection{Error Distribution}

Table~\ref{tab:errors-ood} breaks down the error types on Laptop14 for the NL baseline. The model produces 517 predictions against 543 gold triplets, with 278 correct matches.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Error breakdown for the NL baseline on Laptop14 OOD (239 false positives, 265 false negatives).}
\label{tab:errors-ood}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{False Positive Type} & \textbf{Count (\% of FP)} \\
\midrule
Spurious prediction (other) & 83 (35\%) \\
Polarity error (correct span, wrong polarity) & 57 (24\%) \\
Product name treated as aspect & 42 (18\%) \\
Opinion span boundary error & 27 (11\%) \\
Aspect span boundary error & 15 (6\%) \\
Unannotated but valid aspect & 7 (3\%) \\
\midrule
\textbf{False Negative Type} & \textbf{Count (\% of FN)} \\
\midrule
Unseen aspect (never in training) & 152 (57\%) \\
Near-miss aspect (partial span match) & 54 (20\%) \\
Near-miss opinion (partial span match) & 50 (19\%) \\
Missed despite seen aspect & 5 (2\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The dominant OOD error type is \textbf{unseen aspects} (57\% of false negatives). These are aspects that never appeared in the restaurant training data --- terms like ``OS'', ``setup'', ``runs'', ``portability'', ``keyboard''. The model has no training signal for recognising technology-domain aspects in the syntactic positions where they appear in laptop reviews. No training strategy we tested addresses this fundamental vocabulary gap.

The second major OOD error is \textbf{product names as spurious aspects} (18\% of false positives). The model, trained on restaurant reviews where restaurant names are rarely annotated as aspects, encounters laptop reviews where product names (``MBP'', ``Mac Mini'', ``Air'') appear in aspect-like syntactic positions. The model identifies them as aspects and attaches sentiments to them --- a reasonable behaviour that happens not to match the annotation guidelines of the Laptop14 dataset.

Near-miss errors (aspect boundary: 20\%, opinion boundary: 19\%) represent cases where the model identifies the right element but disagrees on span boundaries (e.g., predicting ``bluetooth devices'' vs gold ``integrate bluetooth devices'', or ``screens'' vs gold ``screen''). These are not conceptual failures but boundary ambiguities.

\subsection{Polarity Confusion}

Polarity errors on OOD data show a clear pattern in the confusion matrix. The most frequent confusions are: gold \textit{negative} predicted as neutral (21 cases), and gold \textit{positive} predicted as neutral (16 cases). At first glance this suggests the model becomes overly conservative on OOD data, defaulting to neutral when uncertain. However, examining the actual sentences reveals a different explanation: most of these ``neutral'' gold labels appear in desire/requirement contexts (``I needed a laptop with big storage'') or factual statements about missing features (``There is no HDMI receptacle''). The model predicts negative for missing features and positive for positive-valence adjectives --- interpretations that are arguably more natural than the annotated neutral labels.

The reverse direction is less frequent: neutral→negative (7 cases) and neutral→positive (3 cases). This asymmetry reflects the training distribution --- Rest14 has only 7\% neutral annotations, so the model has limited exposure to neutral contexts. When it encounters the Laptop14 dataset, which has 12\% neutral (often in non-standard contexts like wish-lists), it tends to assign the polarity that the surface-level words would suggest rather than inferring the neutral pragmatic intent.

\subsection{Annotation Quality Issues}

A non-trivial fraction of ``errors'' reflect questionable gold annotations rather than model failures. We identified several recurring patterns:

\textbf{Hypotheticals and counterfactuals.} The Rest14 annotations sometimes treat hypothetical statements as actual opinions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``a spice rub \textit{might have} overwhelmed'' --- annotated as [spice rub, overwhelmed, negative], but this is a hypothetical dismissal (``it would have been bad, so they didn't do it''), not an actual negative opinion about spice rub.
    \item ``herb mix or other sauce \textit{would have done much} to enhance'' --- annotated as [herb mix, to enhance, negative] and [sauce, to enhance, negative]. The reviewer is expressing a wish, not criticising the herbs or sauce themselves.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Neutral annotation in clearly opinionated contexts.} The Laptop14 dataset frequently uses ``neutral'' for statements that most readers would interpret as having clear sentiment:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``It is really thick around the battery'' --- annotated as [battery, thick, \textit{neutral}]. The model predicts negative, which seems more natural given the complaint-like phrasing.
    \item ``There is no HDMI receptacle'' --- annotated as [HDMI receptacle, no, \textit{neutral}]. The model predicts negative. Reporting a missing feature in a review context carries implicit dissatisfaction.
    \item ``Apple removed the DVD drive'' --- annotated as [DVD drive, removed, \textit{neutral}]. The sentence context makes clear this is a complaint.
    \item ``the intel 4000 graphics chipset'' described as ``defective design'' --- annotated \textit{neutral}. The model predicts negative.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Desire/requirement context.} When reviewers describe what they wanted rather than what they experienced, the annotation guidelines label this as neutral:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``I needed a laptop with big storage, a nice screen and fast'' --- [storage, big, neutral], [screen, nice, neutral]. The model predicts positive for ``big'' and ``nice'' because these are inherently positive-valence adjectives in most training contexts.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Debatable polarity.} Some annotations reflect subjective judgement calls:
\begin{itemize}
    \item ``The staff should be a bit more friendly'' --- [staff, friendly, \textit{negative}]. The model predicts positive because ``friendly'' is positive-valence. The negativity comes from ``should be more'' (implying they are not), which requires pragmatic inference.
    \item ``The sushi is cut in blocks bigger than my cell phone'' --- [sushi, bigger, \textit{negative}]. The model predicts positive (bigger could be generous portions). Whether oversized sushi is positive or negative depends on personal preference.
    \item ``Average cake'' --- annotated as \textit{negative}. The model predicts neutral. Whether ``average'' constitutes a negative opinion is debatable.
\end{itemize}

These cases represent irreducible annotation noise. They inflate the gap between reported F1 and true task performance, but affect all methods equally and do not change relative comparisons. We estimate that 5--10\% of Laptop14 annotations involve genuinely ambiguous polarity where the model's prediction is at least as defensible as the gold label.


\section{Data Analysis}

\subsection{Vocabulary Overlap and Performance}

One of the clearest predictors of cross-domain performance is the degree of vocabulary overlap between training and test data. The aspect token overlap between Rest14 training and Laptop14 test is only 8.7\%, compared to 31\% for the Rest14 test set itself. This 3.5$\times$ reduction in shared vocabulary partially explains the 20-point F1 gap between in-domain and out-of-domain evaluation.

The relationship is visible in the vocabulary overlap matrix (Figure~\ref{fig:vocab-overlap}): SemEval datasets share 20--50\% of their aspect vocabulary within the restaurant family, while cross-domain overlap drops to single digits. Opinion token overlap is consistently higher than aspect overlap across all pairs (47\% for Laptop14 vs Rest14 train), which makes sense --- opinion words like ``good'', ``terrible'', and ``excellent'' are largely domain-independent, while aspect terms are inherently domain-specific.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/vocab_overlap.pdf}
\caption{Aspect and opinion token vocabulary overlap between training data and each test set. Aspect overlap is much lower cross-domain than opinion overlap.}
\label{fig:vocab-overlap}
\end{figure}

However, vocabulary overlap alone does not predict performance. Figure~\ref{fig:overlap-f1} plots F1 against several dataset characteristics. The Pet domain has 54.5\% aspect overlap with DMASTE training sources (highest among target domains) but second-worst F1, explained by its 47.4\% implicit aspect rate --- the highest of any domain. Vocabulary overlap is necessary but not sufficient; implicit aspects, sentence length, and annotation density all modulate the relationship.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/overlap_vs_f1.pdf}
\caption{F1 plotted against dataset characteristics: vocabulary overlap, implicit aspect rate, sentence length, and triplets per sentence. Multiple factors interact to determine cross-domain performance.}
\label{fig:overlap-f1}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Linguistic Differences Between Domains}

Beyond vocabulary, there are structural linguistic differences between the annotation styles of different benchmarks that affect transferability. Figure~\ref{fig:pos} compares the part-of-speech distribution of aspect and opinion head tokens across all 13 datasets.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/pos_comparison.pdf}
\caption{POS distribution of aspect heads (top) and opinion heads (bottom) across all datasets. SemEval opinions are ADJ-dominant while DMASTE opinions are VERB-dominant.}
\label{fig:pos}
\end{figure}

The most striking difference is in opinion annotations: SemEval opinions are ADJ-dominant (37--46\% of opinion head POS are adjectives --- ``delicious'', ``horrible'', ``great''), while DMASTE opinions are VERB-dominant (21--24\% verbs --- ``works'', ``love'', ``recommend''). This is not just a vocabulary difference but a fundamental annotation style difference. SemEval annotators tend to select single adjectives as opinion spans, while DMASTE annotators include verb phrases and multi-word expressions. A model trained exclusively on adjective-heavy SemEval opinions will underperform on verb-heavy DMASTE opinions regardless of vocabulary overlap.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\textwidth]{figures/dataset_characteristics.pdf}
\caption{Dataset characteristics comparison: sentence length, triplets per sentence, implicit aspect rate, aspect span length, and opinion span length across all datasets.}
\label{fig:characteristics}
\end{figure}

The dataset characteristics (Figure~\ref{fig:characteristics}) reveal further structural differences. DMASTE sentences are 3--4$\times$ longer than SemEval (55--64 tokens vs 15--17), contain 2$\times$ more triplets per sentence (3.3--4.0 vs 1.5--2.0), and have much higher implicit aspect rates (38--47\% vs 0\% in SemEval). These compounding differences make DMASTE a substantially harder cross-domain target than the near-OOD SemEval restaurant variants.


\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Why Does NL Format Help OOD?}

The +8.6 point OOD improvement from NL format is the largest single finding of this thesis. We believe it works through two mechanisms:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Pre-training alignment}: FLAN-T5 was pre-trained to generate fluent English. The NL templates (``X is described as Y, expressing a Z sentiment'') are within the model's pre-training distribution. On OOD inputs where the model is uncertain about specific aspects or opinions, it can still generate well-formed template text and fill in the slots with its best guess. The structured format ([X, Y, Z]) is an arbitrary syntax that provides no such fallback.
    
    \item \textbf{Implicit structural constraints}: The template words act as barriers against degenerate outputs. A model generating structured output can easily produce malformed brackets or skip elements when uncertain. The NL template's connecting phrases (``is described as'', ``expressing a'') force the model to commit to a complete triplet or produce nothing recognisable.
\end{enumerate}

The evidence against a third hypothesis --- that NL simply provides more training signal through longer outputs --- is that the structured format generates fewer tokens but the same number of information-bearing elements per example. If longer output sequences alone helped generalisation, we would expect augmentation strategies that increase training volume (sub-task decomposition, multi-ordering) to show similar gains, but they do not. The benefit is specifically from \textit{output-side} alignment with pre-training, not from output length.

\subsection{Why Do Most Interventions Fail?}

The negative results share a common pattern: interventions that increase training variety \textit{within the same domain} do not help when the test domain has fundamentally different vocabulary and annotation style. Curriculum learning, sub-task decomposition, masking, and multi-ordering all produce more diverse training examples, but the diversity is over task structure, not over domain content.

Cross-domain data mixing is the one intervention that adds actual domain diversity, yet it still fails for far-OOD (Laptop14). The reason is likely that the DMASTE domains (electronics, beauty, fashion, home) are more similar to each other (Amazon product reviews with similar annotation style) than to the SemEval restaurant domain. Adding more Amazon-style data does not bridge the gap to laptop reviews annotated in SemEval style.

\subsection{Implications}

For practitioners deploying generative ABSA models:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Use natural-language output templates. This is a free lunch --- no additional data, no additional computation, just a design choice that provides robust OOD gains.
    \item Do not expect syntax enrichment, augmentation, or multi-view prompting to solve domain shift. The problem is fundamentally about vocabulary and annotation style mismatch, not about training signal quantity or model capacity.
    \item Fine-tune a small model rather than prompting a large one. 250M parameters with NL output consistently outperforms 27--32B parameter LLMs in few-shot for extraction tasks.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Conclusions}\label{ch:conclusions}

\section{Summary}

This thesis presented a systematic empirical study of training strategies for out-of-domain generalisation in generative aspect-based sentiment analysis. We evaluated output format, syntactic enrichment, multiple data augmentation strategies, task decomposition methods, and decoding strategies, measuring their OOD impact with multi-seed statistical validation across three benchmarks.

The main finding is that natural-language output format is the single most effective lever for OOD generalisation, providing +8.6 F1 points over structured output with non-overlapping confidence intervals. This is a design choice that costs nothing in terms of additional data or computation, yet delivers larger OOD gains than any other intervention we tested.

On the DMASTE benchmark, our FLAN-T5-base model with NL output matches the best previously reported cross-domain result (Span-ASTE) without any domain adaptation. On the SemEval benchmark, it outperforms 27--32B parameter LLMs in few-shot settings by 10+ F1 points on OOD data.

Most other interventions we tested --- syntactic enrichment, curriculum learning, aspect masking, cross-domain data mixing, LLM-generated opinion augmentation, training with permuted task orderings (and a faithful replication of MvP's inference-time voting~\cite{gou2023mvp}), sub-task decomposition~\cite{xie2025star}, and alternative decoding strategies --- did not reliably improve OOD performance. These negative results are documented in detail so that future work can avoid repeating them.

\section{Limitations}

This work has several limitations that should be acknowledged:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Single language family}: All ASTE experiments are in English. The Romanian experiments use a different task formulation (polarity+category, not full triplets), so we cannot confirm that the NL format advantage extends to non-English triplet extraction.
    
    \item \textbf{Single model size}: We tested only FLAN-T5-base (250M). It is possible that the NL format advantage diminishes at larger scales where the model has more capacity to learn arbitrary output formats.
    
    \item \textbf{Annotation noise ceiling}: The SemEval benchmarks contain annotation inconsistencies (counterfactuals labelled as opinions, disagreeable polarity assignments) that affect all methods equally but make it difficult to determine the true performance ceiling.
    
    \item \textbf{Single-seed for some experiments}: While the primary comparisons use 5 seeds, several ablation studies (curriculum, masking, domain mixing) use a single seed due to computational constraints. Results within the baseline's confidence interval cannot be conclusively interpreted.
    
    \item \textbf{DMASTE implicit aspects}: The DMASTE datasets have 40--47\% implicit aspects which our model handles but cannot truly solve --- predicting aspects not mentioned in the text is a fundamentally different problem from extracting mentioned spans.
\end{itemize}

\section{Future Work}

Several directions could extend this work:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Implicit aspect generation}: Using LLMs to rewrite sentences with explicit aspects into versions with implied aspects, then training on both. This would directly address the implicit aspect gap that explains much of the DMASTE difficulty.
    
    \item \textbf{Domain-adaptive pre-training}: Continuing FLAN-T5's pre-training on unlabeled target-domain text before fine-tuning on labeled source-domain data. This could address the vocabulary gap without requiring labeled target data.
    
    \item \textbf{Larger model scales}: Testing whether the NL format advantage persists with FLAN-T5-large (780M) or T5-XL (3B), and whether the augmentation strategies that failed at 250M become effective with more capacity.
    
    \item \textbf{Multi-task cross-domain training}: Combining NL output format with MvP-style multi-dataset training (the recipe behind MvP's 76.08 ID result). If multi-task exposure provides domain diversity, it might compound with the NL format's OOD advantage.
    
    \item \textbf{Better augmentation targets}: Our augmentation attempts modified opinions (synonym replacement) and inputs (masking, data mixing). Augmenting the \textit{aspects} themselves --- generating training sentences with novel aspect terms from target-domain vocabulary --- could address the unseen-aspect problem that accounts for 57\% of OOD errors.
\end{itemize}

